commentaire = A quick learning key for the macroscopic identification of Waxcaps ;;;;;;;;;;;;
Commentaire : http://www.wildrye.info/files/waxcap_key.pdf;;;;;;;;;;;;
Espèces non décrites: H. cinerella ,H. citrinopallida,H. xanthocroa,H. lilacina,H. viola,H. laeta var. flava,H. substrangulata,H. turunda,H. coccineocrenata,H. salicis-herbaceae;;;;;;;;;;;;

champ= group;cap colour;stipe colour;cap surface;stipe surface;gill shape;smell;Complément 1;Complément 2;Complément 3;Notes;Détermination facile;Figures
Généralités;;;;;;;;; ;; ;%hygrocybe-1_D.pdf
H. aurantiosplendens;Orange tones ;Yellow tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;;;;Usually the pileus is a distinctive glowing orange colour in contrast to the yellow stipe which is finely fibrillose. However it can be confused with H. chlorophana which does not have the dry finely fibrillose stipe, or a faded H. punicea . Also specimens with a conical cap can be confused with H. persistens;No;
H. aurantiosplendens; Yellow tones ; Yellow tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Stipe fibrillose;Lamellae narrowly adnate;;Usually the pileus is a distinctive glowing orange colour in contrast to the yellow stipe which is finely fibrillose. However it can be confused with H. chlorophana which does not have the dry finely fibrillose stipe, or a faded H. punicea . Also specimens with a conical cap can be confused with H. persistens;No;
H. calciphila;Orange tones ;Orange tones ; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Dry stipe;No reliable macroscopic character distinguishing taxa. Calciphila restricted to calcareous soils;;; ;
H. calciphila; Red tones;Orange tones ; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Dry stipe;No reliable macroscopic character distinguishing taxa. Calciphila restricted to calcareous soils;;; ;
H. calyptriformis;Violet tones;White tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;;;;The Pink Waxcap is the only species with a pink cap and white stipe;Yes;
H. cantharellus;Orange tones ;Orange tones ; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;no smell;;;;The very decurrent gills and dry squamulose reddish or orange cap is distinctive. Two other species: H. turunda and H. coccineocrenata have a similar appearance but have a dry, blackish squamulose pileus. Neither has so far been recorded in Sussex;Yes;
H. cantharellus; Red tones; Red tones; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;no smell;;;;The very decurrent gills and dry squamulose reddish or orange cap is distinctive. Two other species: H. turunda and H. coccineocrenata have a similar appearance but have a dry, blackish squamulose pileus. Neither has so far been recorded in Sussex;Yes;
H. ceracea; Yellow tones ; Yellow tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Stipe smooth;Lamellae free or adnexed;;The yellow dry cap, adnate to decurrent gills, and small stature is distinctive. Can be confused with H. insipida which is usually more orange and has a wet stipe when fresh;Yes;
H. chlorophana;Orange tones ;Orange tones ;Viscid;Wet ;Any other;no smell;;;;Several other yellow waxcaps could initially be confused with this species, but the lack of a fibrillose stipe and lack of a viscid striate cap can be used to recognise this species.;Yes;
H. chlorophana;Orange tones ;Orange tones ;Viscid;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;;;;Several other yellow waxcaps could initially be confused with this species, but the lack of a fibrillose stipe and lack of a viscid striate cap can be used to recognise this species.;Yes;
H. chlorophana; Yellow tones ; Yellow tones ;Viscid;Wet ;Any other;no smell;;;;; 
H. chlorophana; Yellow tones ; Yellow tones ;Viscid;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;;;;; 
H. chlorophana; Yellow tones ; Yellow tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Stipe smooth;Lamellae free or adnexed;;; 
H. citrinovirens; Yellow tones ; Yellow tones ; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;;;;; 
H. citrinovirens;Greeen tones ;Greeen tones ; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;;;;; 
H. coccinea; Red tones; Red tones;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Stipe not coarsely fibrillose;Pileus with no black coating in centre;;The most common scarlet coloured waxcap and in it’s typical form easily recognised by the scarlet colour and broadly adnate gills.;Yes
H. colemanniana;Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);White tone;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;no smell;Pileus with dark centre (grey, brown) and pale margin;Pileus reddish-orange brown with cream margin, intervenose lamellae with a brownish tinge;;Virtually restricted to the downs, typically coloured specimens are distinctive, but if paler may be confused with H. virginea var. ochraceopallida;Yes
H. conica; Turning black ;Turning black;Lubricous or Dry ;Wet ;Any other;no smell;;;;In fresh state could be confused with other species, but all parts will turn black which is distinctive. The variety H. concia var. chloroides is found on sand dunes in;Yes
H. conica; Turning black ;Turning black; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Wet ;Any other;no smell;;;;In fresh state could be confused with other species, but all parts will turn black which is distinctive. The variety H. concia var. chloroides is found on sand dunes in;Yes
H. flavipes;Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);White tone;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;no smell;Pileus shades of grey throughout;Pileus striate at only margin, stipe yellowish at base;;Sussex but needs to be examined under a microscope to be distinguished; 
H. fornicata;White tones ;White tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;;;;; 
H. fornicata;White tones ;White tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;distinctive smell;;;;; 
H. fornicata;White tones ;White tones ; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;;;;; 
H. fornicata;White tones ;White tones ; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;distinctive smell;;;;; 
H. glutinepes var. rubra; Red tones; Red tones;Viscid;Viscid;Any other;no smell;;;;This small and slender species is easy to identify because of the viscid nature of the cap and stipe. Other small yellow to orange species with a viscid stipe tend to have more decurrent gils (eg H. vitellina, H. insipida0;Yes
H. glutinipes;Orange tones ;Orange tones ;Viscid;Viscid;Any other;no smell;;;;; 
H. glutinipes; Yellow tones ; Yellow tones ;Viscid;Viscid;Any other;no smell;;;;; 
H. helobia; Red tones; Red tones; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;distinctive smell;;;;; 
H. ingrata;Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;distinctive smell;Flesh reddening, faint nitrous smell if any;Pileus buff to dark brown, stipe cream to brownish;;; 
H. insipida;Orange tones ;Orange tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Wet ;Any other;no smell;;;;This is a very variable small species. When fresh the wet stipe is distinctive. The stipe usually has a darker red or orange band just below the gills;No
H. insipida;Orange tones ;Orange tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Wet ; Decurrent;no smell;;;;This is a very variable small species. When fresh the wet stipe is distinctive. The stipe usually has a darker red or orange band just below the gills;No
H. insipida; Red tones; Red tones;Lubricous or Dry ;Wet ;Any other;no smell;Stipe without bitter taste;;;This is a very variable small species. When fresh the wet stipe is distinctive. The stipe usually has a darker red or orange band just below the gills;No
H. insipida; Red tones; Red tones;Lubricous or Dry ;Wet ; Decurrent;no smell;;;;This is a very variable small species. When fresh the wet stipe is distinctive. The stipe usually has a darker red or orange band just below the gills;No
H. intermedia;Orange tones ;Orange tones ; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Coarsely fibrillose stipe;;;A large species easily recognised by it’s squamulose pileus and fibrillose stipe. Faded specimens may be confused with H. citrinovirens which has a yellow-green colour;Yes
H. intermedia; Red tones;Orange tones ; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Coarsely fibrillose stipe;;;A large species easily recognised by it’s squamulose pileus and fibrillose stipe. Faded specimens may be confused with H. citrinovirens which has a yellow-green colour;Yes
H. irrigata;Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);Viscid;Viscid;Any other;no smell;;;;The dark brown/grey colour and viscid cap and stipe when fresh makes is distinctive;Yes
H. lacmus;Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);White tone;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;distinctive smell;;;;; 
H. laeta;Greeen tones ;Greeen tones ;Viscid;Viscid; Decurrent;distinctive smell;;;;Some specimens may have a greenish tint and hence be confused with H. psittacina, however the smell of burning rubber is distinctive;Yes
H. laeta;Apricot tones;Apricot tones;Viscid;Viscid; Decurrent;distinctive smell;;;;Some specimens may have a greenish tint and hence be confused with H. psittacina, however the smell of burning rubber is distinctive;Yes
H. marchii; Red tones; Red tones;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Stipe not coarsely fibrillose;Pileus with no black coating in centre;;; 
H. miniata;Orange tones ;Orange tones ; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Dry stipe;Miniata restricted to non-calcareous soils;;; 
H. miniata; Red tones;Orange tones ; Fibrillose or Squamulose;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Dry stipe;Miniata restricted to non-calcareous soils;;; 
H. mucronella;Orange tones ;Orange tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Stipe with bitter taste if placed on tip of tongue;;;One of several small orange species which can be quite variable. However the bitter taste in the base of the stipe is unique, which can usually be detected just by placing the tip of the tongue on the pileus;No
H. mucronella; Red tones; Red tones;Lubricous or Dry ;Wet ;Any other;no smell;Stipe with bitter taste if placed on tip of tongue;;;One of several small orange species which can be quite variable. However the bitter taste in the base of the stipe is unique, which can usually be detected just by placing the tip of the tongue on the pileus;No
H. nitrata;Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;distinctive smell;Flesh not reddening, strong nitrous smell;;;; 
H. nitrata;Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);White tone;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;distinctive smell;;;;; 
H. ovina;Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;distinctive smell;Flesh reddening, faint nitrous smell if any;Both pileus and stipe dark greyish brown;;; 
H. ovina;Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;;;;; 
H. phaeococcinea; Red tones; Red tones;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Stipe not coarsely fibrillose;Pileus with a black coating in centre, usually dark red, exceptionally yellow;;; 
H. pratensis;Apricot tones;Apricot tones;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;no smell;;;;; 
H. pratensis var. pallida;White tones ;White tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;no smell;Pileus dry and pruinose, not hygrophanous, large fungus;;;The large fruit body with apricot coloured cap and decurrent gills makes this very distinctive;Yes
H. psittacina;Greeen tones ;Greeen tones ;Viscid;Viscid;Any other;no smell;;;;; 
H. psittacina var. perplexa; Red tones; Red tones;Viscid;Viscid;Any other;no smell;;;;Although typically green, populations can be found with yellow or red tones and the fruit may lose it’s green tones if affected by frost.;Yes
H. punicea; Red tones; Red tones;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Large fungus with coarsely fibrillose stipe;;;Our largest Waxcap, may confused with H. coccinea which has a smooth stipe. H. splendidissima has been regarded as a variety, but has a dry cap and smell of honey when drying. Old dry specimens may fade to a yellow colour and may be confused with H. aurantiosplendens.;Yes
H. quieta;Orange tones ;Orange tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;distinctive smell;"Usually yellow pileus with darker, orange lamellae; smell faint but distinctive of squashed bed bugs";;The smell of squashed bed bugs makes this a distinctive species. None of the other medium sized yellow waxcaps have a smell. Also usually has gills with a darker orange colour to the cap;Yes;
H. quieta; Yellow tones ; Yellow tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;distinctive smell;;;;The smell of squashed bed bugs makes this a distinctive species. None of the other medium sized yellow waxcaps have a smell. Also usually has gills with a darker orange colour to the cap;Yes
H. radiata;Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);White tone;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;no smell;Pileus shades of grey throughout;Pileus striate from centre to margin, not yellowish at base;;; 
H. reidii;Orange tones ;Orange tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;distinctive smell;Orange fungus, base of stipe smells like honey when rubbed;;;This medium sized orange species has a distinctive smell of honmey when the stipe is rubbed. The other species which has a similar smell has a scarlet colour (H. splendidissimus);Yes
H. russocoriacea;White tones ;White tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;distinctive smell;;;;The white colour and distinctive smell of strong Russian leather or cedarwood makes this a distinctive species;Yes
H. spadicea;Dark Colours (grey, dark brown, grey-blue);Yellow tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;;;;; 
H. splendissima; Red tones; Red tones;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;distinctive smell;;;;; 
H. subpapillata;Orange tones ;Orange tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Stipe without bitter taste;;;; 
H. subpapillata; Red tones; Red tones;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose;Any other;no smell;Stipe not coarsely fibrillose;Pileus with no black coating in centre;;; 
H. virginea var. fuscescens;White tones ;White tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;no smell;Pileus +- lubricous, hygrophanous;Pileus buff, pinkish buff, at least in centre;;; 
H. virginea var. ochraceopallida;White tones ;White tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;no smell;Pileus +- lubricous, hygrophanous;Pileus buff, pinkish buff, at least in centre;;The cap colour is intermediate between H. virginea var. virginea and H.;Yes

H. virginea var. virginea;White tones ;White tones ;Lubricous or Dry ;Dry or Fibrillose; Decurrent;no smell;Pileus +- lubricous, hygrophanous;Pileus white;;;
